  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 132 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe ridge continues to flatten today, causing increased westerly \nflow aloft. These winds have been mixing down to the surface, \ncausing breezy conditions, which are expected to dissipate this \nevening. Today will be the warmest day of the forecast period, \nwith multiple locations seeing forecasted highs in the low to mid \n80s. Tomorrow upper level ridging once again forms, with the axis \nof the ridge centered off to the west. This will cause \nnorthwesterly flow aloft, and continue to support warm and dry \nconditions through the rest of the work week. \n\nConfidence is increasing in a pattern change beginning on \nSaturday, with a large upper level trough digging southwest from \nthe Olympic Peninsula. This will bring in much colder air and \nincreased moisture. The combination of the colder temperatures and\nincreased moisture will create the possibility of snow. \nSubsequently, snow levels will decrease, and there is the \npotential for the snow to reach the lower elevations. However, \nthere is still a great deal of uncertainty with this system and \nhow the snowfall potential will play out. See the Forecast \nConfidence & Scenarios section for more details. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nConsistency between models regarding a pattern change have \nincreased, therefore increasing confidence that colder \ntemperatures and snowfall will occur. However, there is still a \nlarge amount of uncertainty in how cold it gets and how much snow \nfalls. Models are still divided on the position and timing of the \ntrough, which is where the main source of the uncertainty lies. As\nof the issuance of this AFD, there is now a 60-75% chance of 1\" \nof snow across lower elevations and a 40-60% chance of 3\" of snow \nacross lower elevations. Currently, the models are showing Central\nMT receiving the most lower elevation snow. -Dzomba",
